blocks.text:
**Telling** names the feeling: *She was very sad.* **Showing** gives the reader the evidence and lets them feel it: *She stared at the unopened letter until her tea went cold.* We never read the word *sad*, but we know, and we feel it more because we worked it out ourselves.
Ways to show:
- **actions** and small habits (*he checked the lock twice*);
- the **body** (*her hands wouldn't stay still*);
- **objects** (*the unopened letter*, *the cold tea*);
- **dialogue**, and what a character **avoids** saying (*'Fine,' he said, and changed the subject*).
**When telling is right.** Showing takes space. Use telling for **summary** and **transitions** (*Three years passed.* *The rest of the meeting was routine.*), for background and for small details. Show the moments that matter; tell the bits in between. And once you've shown a feeling, don't explain it again: don't add *She was very sad* after the cold tea.

id.text:
**Telling** (memberi tahu) menyebut perasaannya: *She was very sad.* **Showing** (menunjukkan) memberi pembaca buktinya dan membiarkan mereka merasakannya: *She stared at the unopened letter until her tea went cold.* Kita tidak pernah membaca kata *sad*, tetapi kita tahu, dan kita merasakannya lebih kuat karena kita sendiri yang menyimpulkannya.
Cara menunjukkan:
- **tindakan** dan kebiasaan kecil (*he checked the lock twice*);
- **tubuh** (*her hands wouldn't stay still*);
- **benda** (*the unopened letter*, *the cold tea*);
- **dialog**, dan hal yang **dihindari** seorang tokoh untuk dikatakan (*'Fine,' he said, and changed the subject*).
**Kapan telling itu tepat.** Showing butuh ruang. Pakai telling untuk **ringkasan** dan **peralihan** (*Three years passed.* *The rest of the meeting was routine.*), untuk latar belakang dan detail kecil. Tunjukkan momen-momen yang penting; ceritakan saja bagian di antaranya. Dan setelah kamu menunjukkan sebuah perasaan, jangan jelaskan lagi: jangan tambahkan *She was very sad* setelah teh yang dingin itu.
